            id="ck-rules"
            name="Checklist"
            path="src/components/case-study/Checklist.astro"
            when="Any list in a case study. You don't pick the layout — the content does (labels, bodies, quotes); `layout` only opts into the five alternative looks. Write the copy, then check which branch it falls in."
            code={code.ck}
          >
            <ol class="ds-rules">
              <li>
                <strong>Research → Scene.</strong> <em>Every</em> item has a non-empty <code>sub[]</code> → a pinned
                <code>Scene</code>, one beat per item (label = mono tag, body = caption with a trailing “:” turned into “.”,
                <code>sub</code> = the quotes set large). Wins over everything, including an explicit <code>layout</code>.
              </li>
              <li><strong><code>layout: "quotes"</code></strong> → pull-quote list. Label + body are joined into one quote; leading/trailing quote marks are stripped (a teal “ is drawn).</li>
              <li>
                <strong>Title row</strong> (decided before the rows are read): more than one item, the <em>first has no body</em>, and some later item
                has one → item 1 is removed from the rows and shown as a mono title. If that first label starts with a figure
                (<code>+20 pilots in …</code>, regex <code>^[+-−]?\d[\d.,]*(%|x|×|k)? text</code>) it becomes a <em>headline figure</em> instead —
                <span class="ds-warn">but the figure is only rendered by the pillars layout; in any other branch it is silently dropped</span>.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Ratio rows</strong>: <em>every</em> row label starts <code>N/D</code> with D ≤ 20 (<code>8/10 users …</code>). A lowercase body
                continues the sentence (joined); a capitalised body is a separate line. Then, by <code>layout</code>:
                <ul>
                  <li><code>"flow"</code> → alluvial ribbons — needs &gt; 1 row and <em>one shared D</em>; otherwise falls back to the pictogram.</li>
                  <li><code>"chart"</code> → bar chart widget, sorted by value, “users …” prefix dropped; “n = D” head when all D match.</li>
                  <li><code>"rings"</code> → a progress ring per stat. <code>"cards"</code> → stat cards with a 2×5 people grid.</li>
                  <li>no layout → pictogram rows (one person per D).</li>
                </ul>
                D &gt; 20 is not a ratio: it falls through to metric rows.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Metric rows</strong>: ≥ 2 rows and <em>every</em> label starts with a figure (<code>69% less pages:</code>,
                <code>-37% …</code>, <code>2x …</code>). The figure is set large and thin. A <em>single</em> figure row is not a metric (→ plain list).
                <code>layout: "chart"</code> also accepts metrics when every figure ends in <code>%</code>.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Pillars</strong>: 2–4 rows and <em>every</em> row has a body → hairline grid with 01/02 numbers (3 → three columns, 2/4 → two).
                <code>art</code> renders only here (PillarArt above the number). If every body ends in “?” they're set large as questions.
                A lowercase body joins the title.
              </li>
              <li><strong>Plain list</strong> — everything else: black check discs, bold label + muted body.</li>
            </ol>
            <p class="ds-note">
